<!-- Initializing Package.json -->

npm init -y creates a package.json file. This file records your project’s name, scripts, and dependencies (the libraries it needs). Anyone who gets your project can run npm install and get everything.

<!-- What Express and Nodeman is  -->
express is the framework.
nodemon restarts your server automatically whenever you save a file. It’s installed as a dev dependency because it’s only needed while developing.

What each part does:

<!-- Server Js code explanation  -->
require('express') imports the library.
express() creates your app (the server).
app.get('/', ...) defines a route: when someone sends a GET request to /, run this function.
req is the incoming request (data from the client). res is the response you send back.
app.listen starts the server on port 3000.

Run it with npm run dev, then visit http://localhost:3000 in your browser. You should see your message.


<!-- Understanding Routes and HTTP COMMANDS -->

Step 6: Understand routes and HTTP methods

APIs use different HTTP methods for different actions:

Method	Purpose	Example
GET	Read data	Get all tasks
POST	Create data	Add a new task
PUT/PATCH	Update data	Edit a task
DELETE	Remove data	Delete a task

A route is a combination of a method + a path (like GET /tasks).

Step 7: Add middleware

Middleware is a function that runs between the request arriving and your route handling it. Think of it as a checkpoint. Add this above your routes in server.js:

:id is a route parameter, available as req.params.id.
req.body holds data the client sent.
res.status(404) sets the HTTP status code (200 OK, 201 Created, 400 Bad Request, 404 Not Found, 500 Server Error).
res.json() sends JSON 

